description:
ADR-0018 §The unmatchable state, and decision 4. Availability and the OpenTo* flags COMPOSE: declaring availability marks a contributor available for every flag they have enabled and for nothing else, so a match needs a live window AND the flag for that shape.

That makes one state reachable by accident and undiagnosable from outside — a live window with no flag set. Refresh the window, believe you are findable, and be invisible to every role, with no response anywhere that says so. Decision 12 makes it the DEFAULT: flags are false, because a profile nobody filled in should surface nobody, which means every contributor who has never opened the form is in it.

So `matchable` is a named field on the profile read rather than something a client derives, and this case walks every way of being unmatchable:

  * a live window and no flags (alice) — the accidental state, and the default one;
  * a LAPSED window and all four flags (carol) — the flags do not lapse separately, decision 5: one clock governs both halves, so ticking every box changes nothing while the window is dead;
  * not_looking and a flag (bob) — an explicit opt-out is not a live window, ADR-0008 §1a;
  * no availability row at all (a contributor who signed in a minute ago).

And both repairs: ticking one shape, and refreshing the window. Each flips the field on its own and neither is sufficient alone — which is the whole reason availability travels on the profile read instead of the client joining two calls and getting the rule slightly wrong in each place it asks.

The flags surviving carol's availability write is decision 11 made visible: two tables with two lifetimes, so writing one cannot clear the other.

Every body here also carries `needs_attention` (ADR-0018 amendment 2), and the point of it is the gap between the two fields. `needs_attention` is true ONLY for somebody who has never saved the form; `matchable` is false for several people who have. Carol answers every question, ticks all four shapes, and is still unmatchable because her window is dead — needs_attention false, matchable false. A screen that derived the prompt from `matchable` would nag her about a decision she had already made, and 'I am not open to any of these right now' is a real answer somebody may mean. The two fields agree only for the contributor who has never been asked.
